-- title:
--   Theorem 5.1, p. 34 — Algorithm II either detects infeasibility/LICQ failure or its LICQ limit points are KKT points of min f s.t. g ≤ 0
-- statement:
--   Suppose that the iterates are generated by Algorithm II (barrier parameters $\mu_l$ with $\mu_{l+1}\in(0,a\mu_l)$, tolerances $\varepsilon_l\downarrow0$, inner runs of Algorithm I stopped by (5.1)–(5.2)), and that for each barrier problem on which Algorithm I runs forever, Assumptions 4.1 hold. Then:
--
--   - **(A)** If for some parameter $\mu_l$ the inner run never satisfies (5.1)–(5.2), then along that run: if inequality (5.1) $\|g_k+s_k\|\le\varepsilon_l$ is never satisfied, the stationarity condition for minimizing $x\mapsto\|g(x)^+\|$ holds in the limit, $A(x_k)g(x_k)^+\to0$; and if $g_k+s_k\to0$ (so that (5.2) is never satisfied), the sequence $\{(g_k,A_k)\}$ has a limit point $(\bar g,\bar A)$ failing the linear independence constraint qualification.
--   - **(B)** If at each outer iteration $l$ the inner algorithm finds a pair $(x_{k_l},s_{k_l})$ satisfying (5.1)–(5.2), then all limit points $\hat x$ of $\{x_{k_l}\}$ are feasible, $g(\hat x)\le0$, and if a limit point $\hat x$ satisfies the linear independence constraint qualification, the first-order optimality conditions of $\min f(x)$ s.t. $g(x)\le0$ hold at $\hat x$: there exists $\hat\lambda\in\mathbb R^m$ with
--   $$\nabla f(\hat x)+A(\hat x)\hat\lambda=0,\qquad g(\hat x)\le0,\qquad\hat\lambda\ge0,\qquad g(\hat x)^\top\hat\lambda=0.$$
--
--   This is the global convergence theorem of the overall interior-point method for (2.1).
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Smooth" (p. 1) is read as $C^1$: $f$ and $g$ are continuously differentiable on all of $\mathbb R^n$. Vectors live in `EuclideanSpace`, stacked vectors $(a,b)$ in `WithLp 2` products, so every $\|\cdot\|$ is the Euclidean norm (spectral norm for matrices). $A(x)$ is the adjoint of the derivative of $g$, so $A(x)^\top d_x$ is `fderiv ℝ g x d_x`. Outcome (A) is stated as two implications, one per printed case, for every outer iteration whose inner run is infinite; the printed case split itself is not asserted. Assumptions 4.1 are required only for the inner runs that are infinite, which are the barrier problems the proof uses them for; outcome (B) does not use them. Outer iterations are numbered from $0$.
-- source:
--   Byrd, Gilbert, Nocedal, A trust region method based on interior point techniques for nonlinear programming, INRIA RR-2896 (1996), HAL inria-00073794v1, p. 34, Theorem 5.1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BarrierTR_Global_AlgorithmII
open scoped RealInnerProductSpace
open Filter Topology

namespace BarrierTR.Global

/-- Theorem 5.1 (p. 34). Let Algorithm II be run, and for each barrier problem on which Algorithm I
runs forever let Assumptions 4.1 hold.
(A) If, for some `μ_l`, the inner run never satisfies (5.1)–(5.2): if (5.1) is never satisfied then
`A(x_k) g(x_k)⁺ → 0` along that run; if `g_k + s_k → 0` along that run then `{(g_k, A_k)}` has a
limit point failing the linear independence constraint qualification.
(B) If every outer iteration finds a point `(x_{k_l}, s_{k_l})` satisfying (5.1)–(5.2), all limit
points `x̂` of `{x_{k_l}}` are feasible, and at any such `x̂` satisfying the LICQ the first-order
optimality conditions of `min f(x) s.t. g(x) ≤ 0` hold. -/
theorem theorem_5_1 {n m : ℕ} (f : E n → ℝ) (g : E n → F m) (hf : ContDiff ℝ 1 f)
    (hg : ContDiff ℝ 1 g) (μ ε : ℕ → ℝ) (a : ℝ) (x₁ : E n) (s₁ : F m)
    (P : ℕ → Params n m) (R : ℕ → RunData n m) (K : ℕ → ℕ∞)
    (hII : IsAlgIIRun f g μ ε a x₁ s₁ P R K)
    (hA41 : ∀ l, (∀ j < l, K j ≠ ⊤) → K l = ⊤ → Assumptions41 f g (R l)) :
    (∀ l, (∀ j < l, K j ≠ ⊤) → K l = ⊤ →
      ((∀ k, ε l < ‖g ((R l).x k) + (R l).s k‖) →
          Tendsto (fun k => A g ((R l).x k) (pos (g ((R l).x k)))) atTop (𝓝 0)) ∧
        (Tendsto (fun k => g ((R l).x k) + (R l).s k) atTop (𝓝 0) →
          HasLICQFailingLimitPoint g (R l).x)) ∧
    ((∀ l, K l ≠ ⊤) → ∀ xhat : E n,
      MapClusterPt xhat atTop (fun l => (R l).x (K l).toNat) →
        (∀ i, g xhat i ≤ 0) ∧ (LICQAt g xhat → IsKKTPoint f g xhat)) := by sorry

end BarrierTR.Global
